PART 4

The toolchain
Four tools that do the actual video work. Claude drives them; you just have to put them on the machine once.

You do not need a terminal app for this. Open the terminal pane inside Claude with Ctrl + ` and paste the commands there. Or skip the pasting entirely: in the Code tab, ask Claude to install Homebrew, Node, Python, ffmpeg and uv, then make a Python environment at ~/videotools with rembg, opencv-python-headless, sherpa-onnx and pocketsphinx, and approve each step as it goes.

WHY NODE, WHEN THE APP ALREADY HAS CLAUDE CODE?

The desktop app ships with Claude Code built in, so Claude itself needs nothing. Node.js here is for Remotion — the engine that builds and renders the video. Different job, same machine.

4.1  Homebrew
The package manager for command-line tools on a Mac. Everything else installs through it. Skip if you already have it — check with brew --version.

/bin/bash -c "$(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Homebrew/install/HEAD/install.sh)"


It explains what it will do and pauses for you to confirm. At the end it may print two lines starting with echo >> ~/.zprofile — run those too, then close and reopen the terminal pane.

4.2  Node, Python, ffmpeg, uv
brew install node python ffmpeg uv


Tool

What it does here

Node.js

Runs Remotion — builds the video and renders it

Python

Runs the cut-out and transcription models

ffmpeg

Reads, cuts, converts and muxes video and audio. The workhorse behind everything

uv

Installs Earshot, the sound effects tool


NODE VERSION MATTERS

The skills installer in Part 5 needs Node 22.20 or later. Check with node -v. If Homebrew gave you something older, run brew upgrade node.

4.3  A Python home for the video tools
macOS will not let you install Python packages system-wide any more — you get an externally-managed-environment error. Give the video tools their own environment instead. This one step prevents the most common error people hit.

python3 -m venv ~/videotools

~/videotools/bin/pip install --upgrade pip

~/videotools/bin/pip install "rembg[cpu]" opencv-python-headless sherpa-onnx pocketsphinx


About 1 GB, a few minutes. What each package does is in Part 5.

TELL CLAUDE WHERE THIS IS

At the start of an editing session, say: "the Python video tools are at ~/videotools/bin/python". Better still, put that line in a CLAUDE.md file in your folder (Part 5.10) so you never have to say it again.

4.4  Check it all worked
Paste this one block in the terminal pane. Every line should print a version, not an error.

brew --version && node -v && python3 -V && ffmpeg -version | head -1 && uv --version \

  && ~/videotools/bin/python -c "import rembg, sherpa_onnx, pocketsphinx; print('python tools ok')"


Anything that fails, fix it before moving on. Part 10 has the usual errors and their fixes.


PART 5

The skills and plugins
5.0  What a skill actually is
A skill is a folder with a SKILL.md file in it — a set of written instructions Claude reads when the job matches. That is genuinely all it is. Nothing is installed into Claude itself; you are just leaving notes where it will find them.

On your machine, personal skills live here:

~/.claude/skills/<skill-name>/SKILL.md


Claude loads a skill automatically when your request matches its description, or you can call it directly by typing /skill-name. Type / in the prompt box to see everything that is installed — that is how you verify each step below.

Most of the skills here install with one command, using the open skills CLI. It copies the skill into the folder above.

SKILLS VS PLUGINS

A plugin is a bundle — it can carry several skills, plus agents, hooks and tool connections. In the desktop app you add plugins with the + button next to the prompt box, then Add plugins. Everything in this guide installs as a skill, which is simpler. Where a plugin exists too, I say so.

5.1  Remotion — the engine
What it is: a framework for making videos out of React code. It is the thing that actually draws every frame and renders the MP4. Without it, Claude can describe an edit but cannot produce one.

What it gives you: real motion graphics — text that animates per word, cards that slide in on a spring, a timeline mockup, a phone frame, a number counting up. All drawn as code, at 1080x1920, at whatever frame rate you shot.

Install: you do not install Remotion globally. It lives inside your project. Easiest path — in the Code tab, ask:

Set up a Remotion project in this folder for a 1080x1920, 30fps reel.


If you would rather do it yourself in the terminal pane:

npx create-video@latest


Then add the extra packages Claude uses for transitions, shapes, audio and captions:

npm i @remotion/transitions @remotion/shapes @remotion/paths @remotion/noise \

  @remotion/motion-blur @remotion/media @remotion/media-utils @remotion/captions \

  @remotion/layout-utils


Licence: free for individuals and teams of up to 3 people. A bigger company needs a paid licence. (remotion.dev/license)

5.2  Remotion agent skills — official best practices
What it is: the Remotion team's own set of skills that teach Claude how to write good Remotion code — how to time animations, build captions, handle audio and video, and run a render.

Why it matters: this is the difference between code that works and code that looks right. Without it Claude writes animations that technically run but feel mechanical.

Install:

npx skills@latest add remotion-dev/skills -g -a claude-code


That installs remotion-best-practices, remotion-markup, remotion-captions, remotion-multimedia, remotion-render, remotion-create and more. Published and maintained by the Remotion team.

5.3  Anatomy — isometric explainer graphics
What it is: a skill that draws detailed isometric diagrams as code — a machine, a rig, a system, a process, drawn as a real-looking object with proper shading and animation.

Why I use it: when a line in my script explains how something works, a plain text card is boring. This draws the thing. It is the single biggest reason my reels do not look like everyone else's, and almost nobody is using it for video.

Install:

npx skills add wheresryan22/anatomy -g -a claude-code


Needs: Node 20.10 or later, and Chrome for its visual checks. Licence: MIT. Repo: github.com/wheresryan22/anatomy

5.4  Earshot — the sound effects bank
What it is: two things in one. A bank of 252 sound effects stored as code — clicks, pops, whooshes, risers, impacts, glitches, typing runs, notification dings — that render instantly with no downloads. And a set of ears: it can listen to audio and describe it, which lets Claude check its own mix.

Why it matters: sound is most of what makes an edit feel expensive. The reel in this guide has about 95 sound cues placed frame-accurately against the words. Doing that by hand is hours of work.

Install:

uv tool install "earshot[mcp] @ git+https://github.com/Leonxlnx/earshot"

earshot doctor --fetch


The second line downloads its two listening models, about 1.1 GB. You can skip it if you only want the sound bank — the effects themselves need no models and no network.

Then add its two skills so Claude knows how to use it:

git clone https://github.com/Leonxlnx/earshot ~/earshot-src

mkdir -p ~/.claude/skills

cp -r ~/earshot-src/skills/earshot ~/earshot-src/skills/real-sound ~/.claude/skills/


There is one more optional step — connecting Earshot to Claude as a tool, so Claude can listen to a file on request rather than just placing sounds. It needs the separate terminal version of Claude Code, so skip it unless you have that; the sound bank works fully without it.

claude mcp add earshot --scope user -e EARSHOT_THREADS=3 -- earshot mcp


Licence: MIT. Repo: github.com/Leonxlnx/earshot

5.5  Design and motion skills
What they are: a set of skills by Emil Kowalski, a design engineer who worked at Vercel and Linear, on how motion should actually feel — which easing curve to use, how long an animation should run, what to stagger, and when not to animate at all.

Why it matters: this is taste, written down. It is the reason things land with a soft overshoot instead of sliding in flatly, and the reason the edit does not have an animation on every single element.

Install:

npx skills@latest add emilkowalski/skills -g -a claude-code


The ones that matter for video: apple-design (Apple's motion principles), animate, choreograph (coordinating several things moving at once), animation-vocabulary and review-animations. Licence: MIT. Repo: github.com/emilkowalski/skills

5.6  Thinking Orbs — the "AI is working" animation
What it is: nine animated dot-orbs built for AI interfaces — searching, working, solving, listening, connecting, weaving, composing, breathing, shaping. Drawn on a plain canvas, so they render fast and look identical everywhere.

Why I use it: any shot where Claude is thinking or processing. It reads instantly and beats a spinner.

Install (inside your Remotion project, not globally):

npm install thinking-orbs


One catch worth knowing: the package animates off the browser clock, which does not work in a frame-by-frame render. Ask Claude for "a frame-driven Remotion wrapper around thinking-orbs" and it will write the small adapter that fixes it. Licence: MIT. Repo: github.com/Jakubantalik/thinking-orbs

5.7  rembg — cutting you out of the background
What it is: a background-removal model. It separates you from the room, per frame, so graphics can sit behind you.

Why it matters: this is what makes a title rise up behind your head, or lets you break out of a shrinking reel card. It is the effect that reads as "professionally edited" more than any other, and it needs no green screen.

Install: already done in Part 4.3. The model for people is u2net_human_seg and it downloads itself the first time it runs.

Speed note: it processes each frame individually. Running it on a small copy of the footage and scaling the result up is much faster and looks the same — Claude does this by default. Licence: MIT (model weights carry their own licences).

5.8  sherpa-onnx and pocketsphinx — word-perfect captions
What they are: two speech tools. sherpa-onnx runs a Whisper model locally and writes down what you actually said. pocketsphinx then force-aligns that text against your audio to get the exact start and end time of every single word.

Why both: the first gives you the words, the second gives you the timing. Together they are why the captions in my reels land on the beat instead of drifting, and why Claude knows that "Export" happens at 46.44 seconds and can drop a sound effect exactly there.

Install: already done in Part 4.3. The Whisper model downloads on first use, from the sherpa-onnx releases page.

Licences: sherpa-onnx is Apache 2.0, pocketsphinx is BSD.

