PART 8

What Claude actually does
You do not need to know this to use the workflow. You need it the moment something looks wrong, because then you can point at the step that broke instead of saying "it looks weird".

The eight steps
1 · Makes a working copy
Your 4K footage is too heavy to edit against frame by frame, so it makes a 1080p copy and works on that. The final render still uses full quality.

2 · Transcribes you
Runs a Whisper model locally over your audio and writes down what you actually said — not what your script said. This matters: if you ad-libbed "Easy!" at the end, the captions say "Easy!".

3 · Times every word
Force-aligns that transcript against the audio to get the start and end of each word, to within about a frame. Everything downstream hangs off this: caption changes, animation starts, sound cues.

4 · Finds the look-aways
Runs scene detection over the video to find where the shot changes — which, in a static talking-head take, is exactly where you turned away to read. It gives you those timestamps to confirm.

5 · Plans the scenes
Maps every line of your script to a treatment: face on screen, split screen with a graphic above, or full-screen B-roll. The look-aways become full-screen graphics automatically.

6 · Builds the graphics
Writes the Remotion code for each scene — the Finder windows, the phone mockups, the timelines, the orbs, the counters. This is the slow part, and the part worth reviewing on a still.

7 · Cuts you out, where needed
Runs rembg over just the frames that need it, so graphics can sit behind you.

8 · Sound, render, check
Places the sound cues against the word timings, renders the whole thing, runs a limiter so the audio never clips, then checks the output frame by frame against the cut points.


WHAT TO ASK WHEN SOMETHING IS OFF

Name the step. "The captions drift after 30 seconds" is step 3. "It cut to B-roll while I was still on camera" is step 4. "The graphic looks generic" is step 6. Claude can go straight back to that stage instead of rebuilding everything.


PART 9

Revisions
Your first draft will be 85% right. The last 15% is a conversation, and how you phrase it decides whether you get there in two rounds or ten.

9.1  Be specific, with a timestamp
