PART 11

Limits — the honest list
I am not going to pretend this replaces an editor for everything. Here is where it genuinely is not there yet.

It cannot pick your best take. Trim first. That judgement is yours and it always will be.
It is slow compared to dragging a clip. One small change can mean a re-render. The payoff is that the hundredth change costs the same as the first.
Comedy timing needs you. It will place a cut correctly and still miss the beat that makes a joke land. You have to feel that and ask for it.
It does not find B-roll for you. It builds graphics brilliantly. It cannot go and fetch a stock clip of a city at night — that goes in Resources yourself.
Music has to be yours. Licensed tracks cannot be downloaded automatically. It will cut to your track, not find one.
Long videos get expensive. This workflow is built for 30 to 90 seconds. A ten-minute YouTube video is a different problem.
First reel is slow. Two hours, probably. You are teaching it your style and learning what to ask for. Reel three is forty minutes.

WHAT IT IS GENUINELY BETTER AT THAN ME

Frame-accurate sound design. Ninety-five sound cues placed against individual words is not something I would ever sit and do by hand, and it is most of why the edit sounds expensive.


